  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nAn area of rain showers are spreading into the area from the \nwest this morning, and will steadily spread east through the day\ntoday. This area of rainfall is being driven by a fairly potent\nshortwave which is moving across the region today, together \nwith a narrow plume of gulf moisture to bring us this much \nneeded rain. The highest rainfall amounts, ranging from a \nquarter to a half inch, are expected from the Brainerd Lakes \narea northward toward International Falls. As this precipitation\nencounters high pressure and drier low-level air over \nWisconsin, it will weaken. Therefore, locations across northwest\nWisconsin and for the tip of the Arrowhead will likely remain \ncompletely dry today. High temperatures this afternoon will \nrange from the upper 50s in cloudy and rainy parts of Minnesota \nto the upper 60s in northwestern Wisconsin.\n\nRidging aloft builds over the area for Friday, but with that \nplume of moisture still lingering over the area it will be \npartly to mostly cloudy, but no precipitation is expected. \nSoutherly flow will bring warmer air into the region, and \ndespite the clouds we should get highs in the 60s to low 70s. \nAnother shortwave will ride over the upper level ridge and bring\nus another wave of precipitation Saturday afternoon and night, \nagain mainly for northeastern Minnesota. There may be a few \nrumbles of thunder Saturday afternoon. Rainfall amounts with \nthis wave do not look like they will be very high, perhaps a \ntenth of an inch. Sunday will be another generally quiet day \nwith lingering clouds and perhaps some sprinkles. High \ntemperatures both Saturday and Sunday will be in the 60s for \nmost of the Northland. \n\nEarly next week looks active as an upper level trough and a\nfairly strong surface low pressure system will move out of the\nRockies and across the plains to the Great Lakes. This system\nwill likely bring a swath of widespread, beneficial rainfall to\nthe Northland, probably in the Monday night through Tuesday time\nframe, with some lingering precipitation into Wednesday.\nTemperatures will remain mild for Monday, but then a cooler\nairmass sliding in behind the rainfall will bring high\ntemperatures back down into the upper 50s to mid 60s by\nWednesday.",
